Consider the following generic class declarations:

    abstract public class Either<S,T> {
        public S getLeftOrElse(S def) {return def;}
        public T getRightOrElse(T def) {return def;}
    }

    public class Left<S,T> extends Either<S,T> {
        private S left;
        public Left(S l) { left = l; }
        @Override
        public S getLeftOrElse(S def) { 
            if (left == null)
                return def; 
            else
                return left; }
    }

    public class Right<S,T> extends Either<S,T> {
        private T right;
        public Right(T r) { right = r; }
        @Override
        public T getRightOrElse(T def) { 
            if (right == null)
                return def;
            else
                return right; }
    }

What output will the following code produce:

    Either<String, Integer> e1 = new Left("Froods");
    Either<String, Integer> e2 = new Right(null);

